# Spacing and layout

Spacing uses Tailwind's 4px-based scale through utilities on page
markup. Components carry their own internal padding; pages are
responsible only for the space *between* components.

## Spacing scale

A deliberately small set of steps covers almost every gap:

| Step | Size | Use |
| ---- | ---- | --- |
| `gap-1` / `p-1` | 4px | Icon-to-label inside a control |
| `gap-2` / `p-2` | 8px | Related controls in a row (button groups) |
| `gap-4` / `p-4` | 16px | Between fields in a form, cards in a grid |
| `gap-6` / `p-6` | 24px | Between page sections; card internal padding |
| `gap-8` | 32px | Major page regions |

If a gap is not one of these, round to the nearest step rather than
inventing an in-between value.

## Page structure

- The app shell is the sidebar plus header; page content sits in the
  remaining region with consistent edge padding (`p-6`).
- Lay pages out with flex/grid `gap-*` utilities rather than margins
  on children — spacing belongs to the container, so components stay
  reusable anywhere.
- Cards are the unit of page composition: group related content into
  cards, then grid the cards (`grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2` and
  similar).
- Forms are single-column by default; side-by-side fields only for
  genuinely paired values (date ranges, structured names).

## Rules

- Never add margin inside a component partial to position it on a
  page; pass nothing — the page's container gap does the positioning.
- Prefer `gap-*` over `space-y-*`, and either over child margins.
- Breakpoint prefixes (`md:`, `lg:`) adjust column counts, not
  spacing steps — gaps stay the same across screen sizes.
- Width constraints (`max-w-*`) live on the page region, not on
  components.
